Let $f(x,y,z)=x^4+4y^2+4z^4$ with constraint $g(x,y,z)=xyz-2=0.$ By Lagrange multipliers, at an extremum we must have $$\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g.$$ This gives us the system of equations $$\begin{cases} 4x^3=yz\\ 8y=0\\ 8z^3=x \end{cases}$$ The second equation implies $y=0,$ but this contradicts the positivity condition on $x,y,$ and $z.$ Thus there are no critical points in the domain. Hence the minimum value of $f$ occurs on the boundary of the domain. However, since $x,y,$ and $z$ are all positive, the only possible boundary point is $(x,y,z)=(\infty,0,\infty),$ where $f(\infty,0,\infty)=\infty.$ Therefore, the minimum value of $f$ does not exist. \boxed{\text{The minimum value of }x^4+4y^2+4z^4 \text{ does not exist.}}
